   "source": [
    "# Recipe Traffic Prediction\n",
    "\n",
    "A machine learning project developed as part of the DataCamp Data Scientist Professional Certification."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "267efb92-bcd2-427a-b714-9c21ee02a6d1",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Executive Summary\n",
    "\n",
    "This project developed a machine learning model to predict which recipes will drive high traffic to the website, addressing the business goal of correctly identifying popular recipes 80% of the time.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Data:** 947 recipes with nutritional information (calories, carbohydrate, sugar, protein), category, and number of servings. The target variable was whether a recipe drove high traffic when featured.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Key findings from exploration:**\n",
    "- Category is the strongest predictor of traffic: Vegetable (99%), Potato (94%), and Pork (92%) consistently attract high traffic, while Beverages (5%) is a strong negative signal\n",
    "- Nutritional variables show weak but statistically significant associations with traffic\n",
    "- Servings shows little variation in traffic rates across groups\n",
    "\n",
    "**Modelling:** A Logistic Regression model was selected for its performance and interpretability, with a Decision Tree as comparison. Features were scaled and the data was split into 80% training and 20% test sets with stratification.\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src="figures/recipe_traffic_ml_pipeline.png" width="500"/>\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "**Results:** The final model achieves **85% precision** and **78% accuracy** on the test set, exceeding the 80% precision business target. Cross-validation confirms the results are consistent and the model generalizes well.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Recommendation:** Deploy the model to screen recipes before featuring them on the homepage, prioritizing Vegetable, Potato, and Pork categories, and avoiding Beverages. This protects high-visibility slots and supports the targeted 40% traffic uplift."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "776e4371-9ee6-4c4b-baa1-32476b17e246",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Data Validation\n",
    "\n",
    "This data set has 947 rows, 8 columns. I have validated all variables and made some changes as described below:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **recipe**: 947 recipes without missing values; numeric data, unique recipe identifier; no cleaning is needed.\n",
    "  \n",
    "- **calories**, **carbohydrate**, **sugar**, **protein**: numeric values, same as the description; all four columns share the same 52 missing values, occurring in the same rows. Missing values were imputed using the median per category.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **category**: 11 categories, no missing values. One extra category not present in the description: 'Chicken Breast', which analysis showed to be a meaningful and distinct group from 'Chicken'.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **servings**: initially categorical column (not numeric as in the description), indicating number of servings of the recipe; originally 6 unique values, with no missing values. '4 as a snack' and '6 as a snack' were merged into '4' and '6' respectively, resulting in 4 final categories, which were then encoded as numeric.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **high_traffi**c: character, indicating if traffic was high when the recipe was shown; only one unique value is registered ('High'); 373 missing values were interpreted as 'Low' and filled accordingly, making it a binary column."
   ]
  },
