   "id": "b45686f9-809d-4f4e-b4ec-76a715617995",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Feature Importance - Observations**\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Category is by far the most influential predictor** of high traffic in both models, with nutritional variables and servings playing a minor role.\n",
    "- The most impactful categories across both models are **Beverages, Vegetable, Potato, Breakfast, and Pork**, consistent with EDA findings.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why do the models disagree on which categories matter?**\n",
    "- **Logistic Regression** coefficients measure how much each feature changes the log-odds of high traffic, giving a global view of both positive signals (Vegetable, Potato, Pork) and negative signals (Beverages).\n",
    "- **Decision Tree** importance asks instead: *\"Does splitting on this feature improve node purity?\"* With `max_depth=4`, the tree found it could separate classes well using Beverages, Breakfast, Chicken, Chicken Breast, and nutritional variables, leaving Vegetable, Potato, and Pork with zero importance.\n",
    "- Zero importance in the tree **does not mean those features are irrelevant** -- it means the depth-4 tree did not select them for its splits.\n",
    "- The scales are also not directly comparable: Decision Tree importances sum to 1, while Logistic Regression coefficients do not. The meaningful comparison is which features each model considers influential, not the numerical values.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Conclusion:**\n",
    "- Logistic Regression provides the more complete and interpretable picture, capturing both ends of the spectrum and aligning with EDA, making it the preferred model for business interpretation.\n"
   ]
  },
